The **intersection** $A \cap B$ is the set of elements in **both** $A$ and $B$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,4.8);
\begin{scope}
\clip (2.9,2.4) circle (1.9);
\fill[blue!15] (5.1,2.4) circle (1.9);
\end{scope}
\draw (2.9,2.4) circle (1.9);
\draw (5.1,2.4) circle (1.9);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.15,4.7) {$\xi$};
\node at (1.9,3.7) {$A$};
\node at (6.1,3.7) {$B$};
\node[below] at (4,-0.15) {$A \cap B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

The **union** $A \cup B$ is the set of elements in **either** $A$ or $B$, with each element listed once.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,4.8);
\fill[blue!15] (2.9,2.4) circle (1.9);
\fill[blue!15] (5.1,2.4) circle (1.9);
\draw (2.9,2.4) circle (1.9);
\draw (5.1,2.4) circle (1.9);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.15,4.7) {$\xi$};
\node at (1.9,3.7) {$A$};
\node at (6.1,3.7) {$B$};
\node[below] at (4,-0.15) {$A \cup B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two sets are **disjoint** (or **mutually exclusive**) when they share no elements, so $A \cap B = \varnothing$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,4.8);
\draw (2.2,2.4) circle (1.6);
\draw (5.8,2.4) circle (1.6);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.15,4.7) {$\xi$};
\node at (2.2,3.5) {$A$};
\node at (5.8,3.5) {$B$};
\node[below] at (4,-0.15) {$A \cap B = \varnothing$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Inside a universal set $\xi$, the **complement** $A'$ (also $\overline{A}$ or $A^c$) is the set of elements not in $A$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[blue!15, even odd rule] (0,0) rectangle (8,4.8) (4,2.4) circle (1.9);
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,4.8);
\draw (4,2.4) circle (1.9);
\node[anchor=north west, font=\small] at (0.15,4.7) {$\xi$};
\node at (4,2.4) {$A$};
\node[below] at (4,-0.15) {$A'$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
